# Project Design Principles — {project-name}
> Set by: `/discover` on first run. Update as new principles are discovered through real work.

> These principles are extracted from this codebase during `/discover` and maintained as work reveals new ones.
>
> They are **guides for judgment** — not hard blocks. When work genuinely requires departing from a principle, document the departure and the reason in the work item's "Design approach" section.
>
> An undocumented departure is a problem. A documented, justified departure is a decision.

---

## How to use these principles

During **`/start`** (planning):
- Check whether the proposed design aligns with or departs from each relevant principle.
- If a departure is needed, capture it explicitly in the "Design approach" section of the work item.

During **`/challenge`** (stress-testing):
- Look for plan steps that conflict with principles without documenting why.
- Flag undocumented conflicts as findings in the "Knowledge conflicts" dimension.

During **`/code-review`**:
- Use these principles to ground review comments: "This departs from [principle name] because..."

